WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.421
A BIN table takes 50 ms to load, so you build it on first use.

2
00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:12.184
A synchronized getter is safe, but then every call takes the lock, forever.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.709
Double-checked locking skips the lock once the table exists.

4
00:00:16.709 --> 00:00:18.169
Check without the lock.

5
00:00:18.169 --> 00:00:20.963
If it is null, lock, check again, and build.

6
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:27.224
Thread A finds null, takes the lock, checks again, and allocates the table, with ranges still null.

7
00:00:27.224 --> 00:00:31.996
In the code, the constructor fills ranges, then the reference goes into table.

8
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.013
But B will read table outside the lock, with a plain read.

9
00:00:36.013 --> 00:00:39.761
That read has no happens-before edge to anything A wrote.

10
00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:43.377
So the JIT and the CPU may let the reference out first.

11
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:44.709
A writes table.

12
00:00:44.709 --> 00:00:47.371
Right then, thread B calls binTable.

13
00:00:47.371 --> 00:00:51.586
table is not null, so B skips the lock, and reads ranges.

14
00:00:51.586 --> 00:00:52.843
What does it see?

15
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:55.432
null.

16
00:00:55.432 --> 00:00:58.501
A table that is not null, and still empty.

17
00:00:58.501 --> 00:01:01.935
A's write lands a moment later, too late for B.

18
00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:09.438
A jcstress test on AArch64 saw this 1.8 million times in 2.3 billion samples.

19
00:01:09.438 --> 00:01:13.593
On x86 it rarely shows, so it seems to work.

20
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.505
The fix is one word: make table volatile.

21
00:01:17.505 --> 00:01:22.841
Now writing table is a release: everything A wrote before it is visible first.

22
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:28.812
And B's volatile read is an acquire: if it sees the reference, it sees ranges too.

23
00:01:29.833 --> 00:01:32.841
For a static field there is a simpler way.

24
00:01:32.841 --> 00:01:34.775
Let the JVM do the locking.

25
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.105
The JVM initializes a class right before its first use, once, under its own lock.

26
00:01:41.105 --> 00:01:46.381
A's first call touches Holder, so the JVM builds the table right then.

27
00:01:46.381 --> 00:01:47.436
B has to wait.

28
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:52.169
Then every thread that uses the class sees what the initializer wrote.

29
00:01:52.169 --> 00:01:54.741
Your code needs no volatile and no lock.

30
00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.984
In Java it is a nested class.

31
00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:01.348
In Kotlin, an object, built on first access.

32
00:02:02.167 --> 00:02:02.744
The rule.

33
00:02:02.744 --> 00:02:06.721
A thread that skips the lock needs its own edge to the writes.

34
00:02:06.721 --> 00:02:10.827
A volatile field gives it one, and so does class initialization.

35
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.077
Check yourself.

36
00:02:12.077 --> 00:02:15.973
Thread B reads a non-null table without taking the lock.

37
00:02:15.973 --> 00:02:19.034
Is it sure to see ranges with a plain field?

38
00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:20.425
With a volatile one?

39
00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:25.245
Plain: no, it can see null.

40
00:02:25.245 --> 00:02:26.965
Volatile: yes, always.
